.3Ã— *tail only* | preserved (no commits/checkpoint change; merge is order-independent by stable-id, collectFindings:678-694) | still RÃ— (both reviewers draw the window) |\n| 2 | **Reword the topology rule** â†’ *\"No parallel agent may MUTATE a shared, under-specified decision surface\"*; keep it an unconditional bright line | **SHIP** | n/a (accuracy) | strengthens it | none |\n| 3 | **Gate âˆ¥ review overlap** (start read-only reviewers while the off-quota `spawnSync` test gate runs) | **MEASURE then maybe** | overlaps off-quota CPU with on-quota model â€” additive IF gate is slow vs review | no commits / no model-call increase | none added |\n| 4 | **Reduce mean fix-iterations *k*** (better EXECUTE prompt / stronger model for EXECUTE only) | **PURSUE (non-parallel)** | ~33% (k 2â†’1); multiplier on the whole cycle | preserved | **fewer** tokens |\n| 5 | **Cheaper/faster SECOND reviewer model** (see Open Questions) | **PURSUE â€” with guards** | ~17â€“22% | preserved | **halves** review tokens |\n| 6 | **Parallelism across *independent projects*** (separate plans/repos/checkpoints) | **VALID, different goal** | real throughput win, zero merge risk | preserved (`worktrees.py` per-session isolation already fits) | linear in projects |\n| 7 | **Wave-DAG / worktree-band parallelism** | **DO NOT SHIP (default)** | ~1.2â€“1.6Ã— only on the minority of plans with independent bands, after merge+re-gate tax | N commit chains forfeit the single-checkpoint/single-branch resume guarantee | higher |\n| 8 | **Speculative/parallel EXECUTE, speculative fixes, cross-wave pipelining** | **REJECT** | small/illusory | violates ascending truth-gate | ~2Ã— â€” anti-lever when quota-bound |\n| 9 | **Shrink the gate of record to \"impacted tests only\"** | **REJECT** | â€” | IS the vacuous-GREEN attack the full-suite gate exists to close | â€” |\n\n**Adjudicated conflict:** an affected-tests pre-check is acceptable ONLY as an inner-loop fast-feedback accelerator â€” the **gate of record stays the full suite** (anti-reward-hacking / vacuous-GREEN invariant).\n\n## WHY PARALLEL-WRITE BRANCHES FAIL (OBSERVED)\n- Disjoint-write-set is an *output* of execution, not a pre-gate â€” collisions are only knowable after running N agents.\n- The conflict is semantic, not file-level (one wave adds `grass_origin=\"\"`, another assumes `None`).\n- The gate is a boolean, not a ranker â€” fan-out needs a ranker to pick among test-passing candidates; Foreman has only GO/NO-GO, so fan-out silently commits design drift.\n- No merge machinery exists (`grep worktree bin/` = nothing); one linear branch, per-file staging, single-`last_commit` reconcile that HALTs if HEAD is >1 commit ahead. Worktree-bands forfeit the durability that makes Foreman trustworthy.\n- R4 (\"declare bands in Crucible\") is under-costed: Crucible's `dependsOn` is `{type:['string','null']}` (single linear predecessor, no DAG/file-set), and zero real plans even populate it.\n\n## CITATION INTEGRITY\n\"Anthropic + Cognition both agree\" = 2 genuinely independent origins (opposite priors, no cross-citation) â€” not a bandwagon. BUT neither has OBSERVED parallel-code-writing data; that sub-claim is CORROBORATED-by-opinion, not OBSERVED. SKILL.md over-generalizes by citing Anthropic (whose own system parallelizes read-only research for a 90% time cut) as an anti-parallel witness; the well-supported claim is the narrower shared-mutation one (= recommendation #2).\n\n## OPEN QUESTIONS FOR PLANNING (John's concerns about the cheaper-reviewer lever #5)\n\nThese convert #5 from \"swap in a cheap model\" into a guarded design. **Planning should treat these as locked requirements, not options.**\n\n### Q1 â€” \"How do we know a lower-tier reviewer is good enough? We might miss something.\"\n- **The reviewer is NOT the arbiter of correctness â€” the orchestrator-run full-suite test GATE is.** Reviewers are an adversarial *supplement* that catches what tests miss (design drift, missing tests, semantic issues). A weaker reviewer degrades the supplement, not the correctness floor. Tests-pass is unchanged.\n- **Keep ONE reviewer strong; make only the SECOND one cheap.** Because a BLOCKER requires â‰¥2 agreement, a cheap second reviewer can only *fail to confirm* a real blocker (a miss) â€” it can never raise a false blocker alone. You lose a blocker only if BOTH miss. This structurally bounds the downside to \"strong model raised it, cheap model didn't confirm.\"\n- **Gate the swap on a measured metric, not a vibe.** Shadow period: run cheap + strong reviewer together for N waves and compute **recall** = of the blockers the strong reviewer raised, what fraction did the cheap reviewer also raise? Also test against a held-out set of historical known-bad diffs / past blockers (false-negative rate). Promote the cheap tier only if recall â‰¥ a pre-registered threshold; else don't.\n\n### Q2 â€” \"Could it get STUCK on a low tier and never upgrade when a better model ships?\"\n- **Never hard-code a model id.** Resolve the reviewer model from a single config var pointing at a model **tier/policy** (\"reviewer = current cheap-tier\"), resolved at run time â€” same discipline Foreman already uses for `REVIEWER_COUNT`. Pin by *role/tier*, not by version string.\n- **Staleness guard + scheduled re-calibration.** A dated re-calibration job (or a healthcheck check) flags \"reviewer model last calibrated > X days\" or \"a newer model in this tier exists,\" and re-runs the Q1 recall calibration on a model-release trigger so the tier can move up. This is a natural `/schedule` or healthcheck owner.\n- **Net:** the swap is safe only as a *policy with a measured promotion gate and a scheduled re-evaluation*, never a static model pin.\n\n## NET\nForeman should stay single-threaded for ALL write work. Ship concurrent reviewers (latency-tail win) + the reword. Treat the cheap-second-reviewer as a guarded, measured, tier-resolved policy (Q1/Q2). The biggest real wins for \"time to deliverable\" are non-parallel: instrument first, reduce fix-iterations, overlap the off-quota gate with review, and â€” for more deliverables/day â€” run independent projects in parallel.\n\n## SOURCES\n- Anthropic â€” How we built our multi-agent research system: https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/multi-agent-research-system\n- Cognition â€” Don't Build Multi-Agents: https://cognition.com/blog/dont-build-multi-agents\n- OBSERVED Foreman/Crucible source (`C:\\dev\\trio\\â€¦`); real `planning/rnd-v*` plans; prior study `researchPrime/runs/foreman-review-2026-06-19/FINAL-REPORT.md`.\n","numLines":69,"startLine":1,"totalLines":69}}}
